  <data key="d5">1779513351</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Inner Mongolia Plateau">
  <data key="d0">Inner Mongolia Plateau</data>
  <data key="d1">location</data>
  <data key="d2">A plateau located in northern China, part of the Second Step terrain.&lt;SEP&gt;The Inner Mongolia Plateau is the second largest plateau in China, located in the northern border region with strategic defensive significance against northern enemies.&lt;SEP&gt;A plateau formed on the west side of the separating mountain line and north of Yinshan.&lt;SEP&gt;The Inner Mongolia Plateau is part of the semi-arid climate zone.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e0902a5c933272b09fcdd85d68e45240&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4b32dded5fc0150b9f8a563e98f31293&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6540ee1693bb1952ff5f0c0a29a6cfda&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bb7c5877c55d5fba2943b2f7e8e86409</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1779513351</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Loess Plateau">
  <data key="d0">Loess Plateau</data>
  <data key="d1">location</data>
  <data key="d2">One of the four major plateaus in China, distributed on the Second Step.&lt;SEP&gt;The Loess Plateau is a fragmented plateau characterized by loess soil and deep gullies, located south of the Inner Mongolia Plateau and spanning multiple provinces.&lt;SEP&gt;A plateau formed on the west side of the separating mountain line.&lt;SEP&gt;The Loess Plateau is a region contributing to high sediment levels in northern rivers due to vegetation loss.&lt;SEP&gt;The Loess Plateau is largely included in the semi-arid climate zone.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e0902a5c933272b09fcdd85d68e45240&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4b32dded5fc0150b9f8a563e98f31293&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d2a34c49c188ba9a655c3c10d21458c3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6540ee1693bb1952ff5f0c0a29a6cfda&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bb7c5877c55d5fba2943b2f7e8e86409</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1779513351</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Yunnan-Guizhou Plateau">
  <data key="d0">Yunnan-Guizhou Plateau</data>
  <data key="d1">location</data>
  <data key="d2">A plateau acting as a barrier in the southwest, part of the Second Step.&lt;SEP&gt;The Yunnan-Guizhou Plateau is located in southwest China, featuring karst landforms and numerous basins, serving as a secure strategic rear base favorable for guerrilla warfare.&lt;SEP&gt;A plateau region bordering the south of the Sichuan Basin.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e0902a5c933272b09fcdd85d68e45240&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5526035222e5349787e63203c8c751d2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6540ee1693bb1952ff5f0c0a29a6cfda</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1779513351</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Tarim Basin">
  <data key="d0">Tarim Basin</data>
  <data key="d1">location</data>
  <data key="d2">One of the four major basins in China, distributed on the Second Step.&lt;SEP&gt;The Tarim Basin is the largest inland basin in China, surrounded by high mountains and containing the Taklamakan Desert, acting as a natural obstacle against western enemies.&lt;SEP&gt;A basin located south of the Tianshan, corridor, and Beishan mountain lines.&lt;SEP&gt;Tarim Basin is a region in northwest China noted for extreme aridity with less than 50 mm annual precipitation.&lt;SEP&gt;The Tarim Basin is a basin in western China with spring temperatures reaching 12 to 16°C.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e0902a5c933272b09fcdd85d68e45240&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4b32dded5fc0150b9f8a563e98f31293&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6540ee1693bb1952ff5f0c0a29a6cfda&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6b3394b9b4e98f146bc3091517249c5b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a79980cadb14f8eecf17a7424058f80a</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1779513351</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Junggar Basin">
  <data key="d0">Junggar Basin</data>
  <data key="d1">location</data>
  <data key="d2">One of the four major basins in China, distributed on the Second Step.&lt;SEP&gt;The Junggar Basin is the second largest basin in China, situated between the Tianshan and Altai mountains, known as an easy passage for invading forces from Central Asia.&lt;SEP&gt;A basin located north of the Tianshan, corridor, and Beishan mountain lines.&lt;SEP&gt;The Junggar Basin is a geographical basin in China with spring temperatures ranging between 0 and 6°C.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e0902a5c933272b09fcdd85d68e45240&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4b32dded5fc0150b9f8a563e98f31293&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6540ee1693bb1952ff5f0c0a29a6cfda&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a79980cadb14f8eecf17a7424058f80a</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1779513351</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Qaidam Basin">
  <data key="d0">Qaidam Basin</data>
  <data key="d1">location</data>
  <data key="d2">One of the four major basins in China, distributed on the First Step.&lt;SEP&gt;The Qaidam Basin is a deep depression located on the Qinghai-Tibet Plateau, bounded by the Kunlun, Altyn-Tagh, and Qilian mountains.&lt;SEP&gt;A high-altitude basin located between several major mountain ranges, known for its rich mineral reserves and salt lake.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e0902a5c933272b09fcdd85d68e45240&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5526035222e5349787e63203c8c751d2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6540ee1693bb1952ff5f0c0a29a6cfda</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1779513351</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Second Level Stairs">
  <data key="d0">Second Level Stairs</data>
  <data key="d1">location</data>
  <data key="d2">The Second Level Stairs is a topographical zone in China where the major plateaus and basins described are distributed.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e0902a5c933272b09fcdd85d68e45240</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1779513351</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Great Wall">
  <data key="d0">Great Wall</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">A historical artifact acting as a southern boundary marker for the Inner Mongolia Plateau.&lt;SEP&gt;The Great Wall serves as a southern boundary marker for the Inner Mongolia Plateau and a historical fortification.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e0902a5c933272b09fcdd85d68e45240&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6540ee1693bb1952ff5f0c0a29a6cfda</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1779513351</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Taklamakan Desert">
  <data key="d0">Taklamakan Desert</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">The Taklamakan Desert is the largest desert within the Tarim Basin, known as the Sea of Death due to its harsh climate and shifting sand dunes.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e0902a5c933272b09fcdd85d68e45240</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1779513351</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Tianshan Mountains">
  <data key="d0">Tianshan Mountains</data>
  <data key="d1">location</data>
  <data key="d2">The Tianshan Mountains form a northern boundary for the Tarim Basin and separate it from the Junggar Basin.&lt;SEP&gt;A major mountain system dividing Xinjiang and acting as a strategic dependency for the north.&lt;SEP&gt;Part of the northern row of east-west oriented mountain ranges in China.&lt;SEP&gt;A mountain range associated with the formation of Junggar Basin to the north and Tarim Basin to the south.&lt;SEP&gt;The Tianshan Mountains are a mountain range in China where autumn temperatures average below 0°C.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e0902a5c933272b09fcdd85d68e45240&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5526035222e5349787e63203c8c751d2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4b32dded5fc0150b9f8a563e98f31293&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6540ee1693bb1952ff5f0c0a29a6cfda&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a79980cadb14f8eecf17a7424058f80a</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1779513351</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Qilian Mountains">
  <data key="d0">Qilian Mountains</data>
  <data key="d1">location</data>
  <data key="d2">A mountain range forming part of the boundary for the Tibetan Plateau.&lt;SEP&gt;The Qilian Mountains serve as a southern boundary for the Inner Mongolia Plateau and help define the Qaidam Basin.&lt;SEP&gt;A mountain range situated alongside the Kunlun and Altyn Mountains surrounding the Qaidam Basin.&lt;SEP&gt;A mountain range in the west of China, located between Altai and Himalayas in a north-to-south sequence.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e0902a5c933272b09fcdd85d68e45240&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5526035222e5349787e63203c8c751d2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4b32dded5fc0150b9f8a563e98f31293&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6540ee1693bb1952ff5f0c0a29a6cfda</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1779513351</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Kunlun Mountains">
  <data key="d0">Kunlun Mountains</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">A mountain range forming part of the boundary for the Tibetan Plateau.&lt;SEP&gt;The Kunlun Mountains surround the Tarim and Qaidam basins, acting as significant geographical barriers.&lt;SEP&gt;A major mountain range forming part of the boundary of the Qaidam Basin.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e0902a5c933272b09fcdd85d68e45240&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5526035222e5349787e63203c8c751d2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6540ee1693bb1952ff5f0c0a29a6cfda</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1779513351</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Mechanized Units">
  <data key="d0">Mechanized Units</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Mechanized Units refer to military forces utilizing advanced vehicles, which find movement difficult in certain terrains like the Loess Plateau but easier in others like the Junggar Basin.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e0902a5c933272b09fcdd85d68e45240</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1779513351</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Defensive Operations">
  <data key="d0">Defensive Operations</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Defensive Operations are military strategies where specific plateaus like the Inner Mongolia Plateau serve as important battlefields.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e0902a5c933272b09fcdd85d68e45240</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
